  const sources = [
    { id: "who-lf-2024", text: "World Health Organization. Lymphatic filariasis fact sheet, 21 November 2024 (caused by parasitic roundworms spread by mosquitoes; adult worms nest in the lymphatic vessels and disrupt the normal function of the lymphatic system; in 2023, 657 million people in 39 countries were living in areas that require preventive chemotherapy to stop the spread of infection; the global baseline estimate of people affected was 25 million men with hydrocele and over 15 million people with lymphoedema).", url: "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/lymphatic-filariasis" },
  ];

  const summaryHTML = () => `
    <h2>How the ideas fit together</h2>
    <p>This chain starts in Unit 1 and runs through <strong>3.2.6</strong>. A large mammal has a small surface area to volume ratio, so diffusion across its body surface could never supply its cells; it uses <strong>mass transport</strong> in a circulatory system (3.1.5.1). The heart generates pressure (3.2.6.2), arteries carry blood at high pressure, and capillaries bring it within a short distance of every cell (3.2.6.1). Fluid forced out of the capillaries forms <strong>tissue fluid</strong>, through which substances are exchanged with cells by diffusion and facilitated diffusion (3.1.4.2). Haemoglobin loads oxygen in the lungs and unloads it in respiring tissues (3.1.5.3). What the cells then do with that oxygen and glucose, aerobic respiration, is A-level content (3.3) and appears here only as supporting.</p>
    <h3>The chain in six links</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Mass transport</strong>: as size increases, surface area to volume ratio falls, so large organisms need transport systems that carry materials such as oxygen efficiently to their cells. <span class="muted">3.1.5.1</span></li>
      <li><strong>The heart</strong>: the thick muscular wall of the left ventricle generates the high pressure that drives blood into the aorta. Valves open and close passively when the pressure difference across them reverses: the atrioventricular valve closes when ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, and the semilunar valve opens when ventricular pressure rises above arterial pressure and closes when it falls below it. Stroke volume is the volume of blood the ventricle ejects in each beat, and cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume. <span class="muted">3.2.6.2, 3.1.5.3</span></li>
      <li><strong>Arteries, veins and capillaries</strong>: arteries have thick elastic and muscular walls for high pressure; veins have a wide lumen and valves for low pressure; capillaries have walls one cell thick in a branched network. <span class="muted">3.2.6.1</span></li>
      <li><strong>Tissue fluid</strong>: at the arteriole end, hydrostatic pressure forces water and small solutes out through the capillary wall, but not plasma proteins; at the venule end, the lower water potential of the blood (due to plasma proteins) draws water back by osmosis; excess tissue fluid drains into the lymphatic system and returns to the blood. <span class="muted">3.2.6.1, 3.1.4.2</span></li>
      <li><strong>Exchange with cells</strong>: oxygen and glucose move from the tissue fluid into cells, and carbon dioxide out, down concentration gradients; the rate depends on surface area, concentration difference and diffusion distance. <span class="muted">3.2.6.1, 3.1.4.2, 3.1.5.3</span></li>
      <li><strong>Cellular metabolism</strong> <span class="badge">supporting</span>: cells use oxygen and glucose in aerobic respiration and release carbon dioxide; ATP is the immediate source of energy for processes such as active transport (3.1.4.3). The carbon dioxide released lowers haemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, so more oxygen is unloaded where it is needed. The biochemistry of respiration is A-level 3.3. <span class="muted">3.1.4.3, 3.1.5.3</span></li>
    </ol>
    <h3>What examiners reward across these topics</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Explaining structure with function: <em>elastic tissue recoils, smoothing and maintaining pressure</em>; <em>one cell thick, so a short diffusion distance</em>.</li>
      <li>Naming the force: hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out; water returns by osmosis because plasma proteins lower the water potential of the blood.</li>
      <li>Linking exchange to Fick's factors: surface area, concentration gradient and diffusion distance.</li>
      <li>Reading cardiac cycle data: compare the pressures on the two sides of a valve to say whether it is open or closed, and quote the values.</li>
      <li>Calculations with units: heart rate from the length of one cycle, stroke volume from the change in ventricular volume, cardiac output in dm³ (litres) per minute.</li>
      <li>Not over-reaching: say "aerobic respiration releases carbon dioxide"; the stages of respiration are not needed here.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">Cross-references only: control of heart rate (SAN, AVN, bundle of His, Purkyne fibres) is A-level 3.4.8; atheroma and myocardial infarction are 3.2.7.</p>`;

